# `operators::stochastic::IntegralOptions`

Options of the stochastic integral: the convention, `θ`, whether to keep the running integral, and the path axis.

`IntegralOptions` is the argument of [`stochastic_integral`](stochastic_integral.md). It adds the integration convention to the two path fields of [`PathOptions`](PathOptions.md).

## Fields

| Field | Type | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `convention` | `Convention` | `Ito` | Where the integrand is read in each step: `Ito` (left end, `θ = 0`), `Stratonovich` (`θ = ½`), `Klimontovich` (right end, `θ = 1`), or `Theta` (use the `theta` field). |
| `theta` | `double` | `0.0` | The `θ` in `[0, 1]`, read only when `convention` is `Theta`. |
| `cumulative` | `bool` | `false` | Return the running integral at every sample (starting at 0) instead of the terminal value. |
| `path_axis` | `std::int64_t` | `-1` | The axis of the arguments that indexes the samples of one path. |

## Guidance

The defaults give the Itô integral up to the final time on the last axis. Change `convention` deliberately: the choice is part of the model, not a numerical option.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

`convention`. The default is `Ito`; naming `Stratonovich` or `Klimontovich` changes where the integrand is read and so the value, by half the quadratic variation per unit of `θ`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace stochastic = pluss::operators::stochastic;
namespace sde = pluss::subsp::math::sde;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const std::int64_t steps = 2000;
  const sde::WienerPath path = sde::simulate_wiener_path(pluss::make_series(0.0), 0.0, 1.0, steps, 7);
  const pluss::Series times = pluss::make_series(path.times, {steps + 1});
  const math::Function integrand = math::function({"t", "W"}, [](math::Function, math::Function w) { return w; });
  const double w_final = path.w.select(0, steps).item<double>();
  const double qv = stochastic::quadratic_variation()(integrand)({times, path.w}).item<double>();

  for (const auto &[label, convention] : {std::pair<const char *, stochastic::Convention>{"Ito         ", stochastic::Convention::Ito}, {"Stratonovich", stochastic::Convention::Stratonovich}, {"Klimontovich", stochastic::Convention::Klimontovich}}) {
    out.writeln(label, stochastic::stochastic_integral("W", {.convention = convention})(integrand)({times, path.w}).item<double>());
  }
}
```

### Example 2

`theta`. It is read only with `Convention::Theta`; with any named convention it is ignored. Here `θ = 0.5` under `Theta` reproduces Stratonovich, and under `Ito` the same `θ` changes nothing.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace stochastic = pluss::operators::stochastic;
namespace sde = pluss::subsp::math::sde;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const std::int64_t steps = 2000;
  const sde::WienerPath path = sde::simulate_wiener_path(pluss::make_series(0.0), 0.0, 1.0, steps, 7);
  const pluss::Series times = pluss::make_series(path.times, {steps + 1});
  const math::Function integrand = math::function({"t", "W"}, [](math::Function, math::Function w) { return w; });
  const double w_final = path.w.select(0, steps).item<double>();
  const double qv = stochastic::quadratic_variation()(integrand)({times, path.w}).item<double>();

  const auto value = [&](stochastic::IntegralOptions o) { return stochastic::stochastic_integral("W", o)(integrand)({times, path.w}).item<double>(); };
  out.writeln("Theta, 0.5:        ", value({.convention = stochastic::Convention::Theta, .theta = 0.5}));
  out.writeln("Stratonovich:      ", value({.convention = stochastic::Convention::Stratonovich}));
  out.writeln("Ito with theta .5: ", value({.convention = stochastic::Convention::Ito, .theta = 0.5}), "  (ignored)");
}
```

### Example 3

`cumulative` and `path_axis`, as for the path variations: a running integral keeps the path axis, and the axis can be named when the samples are not on the last one.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace stochastic = pluss::operators::stochastic;
namespace sde = pluss::subsp::math::sde;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const std::int64_t steps = 2000;
  const sde::WienerPath path = sde::simulate_wiener_path(pluss::make_series(0.0), 0.0, 1.0, steps, 7);
  const pluss::Series times = pluss::make_series(path.times, {steps + 1});
  const math::Function integrand = math::function({"t", "W"}, [](math::Function, math::Function w) { return w; });
  const double w_final = path.w.select(0, steps).item<double>();
  const double qv = stochastic::quadratic_variation()(integrand)({times, path.w}).item<double>();

  const pluss::Series t3t = times.unsqueeze(1).repeat({1, 2}), w3t = path.w.unsqueeze(1).repeat({1, 2});
  const pluss::Series running = stochastic::stochastic_integral("W", {.cumulative = true, .path_axis = 0})(integrand)({t3t, w3t});
  out.writeln("running integral of two paths stored along axis 0: shape", running.sizes());
  out.writeln("last sample of each:", running.select(0, steps).items<double>());
}
```

